WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.985
Adding another application server is useful only if
application work is the limiting step.

00:00:05.985 --> 00:00:12.369
Let us follow a booking request through the shared
data service and estimate its capacity before

00:00:12.369 --> 00:00:14.098
changing the architecture.

00:00:14.098 --> 00:00:17.889
A repair-booking service runs on six application
servers.

00:00:17.889 --> 00:00:20.882
Each booking needs three database operations.

00:00:20.882 --> 00:00:27.532
If the database sustains 1,800 such operations per
second, the idealized ceiling is 600 bookings per

00:00:27.532 --> 00:00:32.586
second, regardless of how many otherwise idle
application servers are added.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:39.131
This estimate assumes equal operation costs and no
competing workload; measurements must check those

00:00:39.131 --> 00:00:39.915
assumptions.

00:00:39.915 --> 00:00:45.140
Now suppose a frequently read parts catalogue fits
in the database buffer cache.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:51.280
Repeated reads may avoid storage access, but writes,
locks and network transfers still consume

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:51.933
resources.

00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:58.008
Adding disks could improve storage throughput
without fixing a CPU-bound query or a contended

00:00:58.008 --> 00:00:58.465
record.

00:00:58.465 --> 00:01:04.931
Separate durable storage, coordination and query
execution when explaining why the database remains

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.956
busy after the web tier scales.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.130
The relational model separates facts that have
different owners and lifetimes.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:18.641
A useful design exercise is to reconstruct one
screen from those facts, then ask both what it means

00:01:18.641 --> 00:01:20.614
and how much work it requires.

00:01:20.614 --> 00:01:24.363
A bicycle library stores Bicycle, Branch and Loan
tables.

00:01:24.363 --> 00:01:30.677
Each bicycle refers to its current branch, and each
loan refers to one bicycle and one borrower.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:35.810
Renaming a branch changes one branch record rather
than every bicycle description.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:41.854
A loan-history page joins those records to display
useful information without storing a duplicate

00:01:41.854 --> 00:01:43.474
branch name in every loan.

00:01:43.474 --> 00:01:49.580
For a query asking for one borrower’s active loans,
the database can first identify a small set of

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.509
matching loans and then look up their bicycles.

00:01:52.509 --> 00:01:56.318
Reading every bicycle before filtering could do much
more work.

00:01:56.318 --> 00:02:01.760
The execution plan reveals the chosen access path;
the schema alone does not guarantee it.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.564
Also decide whether the page should display the
branch’s current name or preserve its historical

00:02:07.564 --> 00:02:09.257
name when the loan occurred.

00:02:09.257 --> 00:02:13.692
Think of an index as a route through data designed
for a particular question.

00:02:13.692 --> 00:02:19.395
We will choose one for a parcel service and then
account for the extra work that the route creates.

00:02:19.395 --> 00:02:24.982
A parcel service frequently asks for a particular
depot’s shipments created during the last hour.

00:02:24.982 --> 00:02:31.323
An ordered index on (depot_id, created_at) groups
one depot’s entries and then narrows the time

00:02:31.323 --> 00:02:31.924
interval.

00:02:31.924 --> 00:02:38.399
A query for all depots during that hour may need a
different plan because its predicate omits the

00:02:38.399 --> 00:02:39.400
leading column.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.005
The optimizer and data distribution determine the
actual access path.

00:02:44.005 --> 00:02:50.480
Suppose that targeted query examines 240 index
entries instead of scanning 800,000 shipment rows.

00:02:50.482 --> 00:02:55.818
That is a promising reduction in examined data, not
a guaranteed latency ratio.

00:02:55.818 --> 00:02:59.263
Every inserted shipment must also update the index.

00:02:59.263 --> 00:03:06.018
Compare execution plans and measured response times
before and after the change, including ingestion

00:03:06.018 --> 00:03:09.328
throughput, index size and unusually busy depots.

00:03:09.328 --> 00:03:14.529
An index is a maintained copy of ordering
information, not free acceleration.
